        <mxCell id="_59ODELO9mQjOOhbrNxv-29" value="&lt;b&gt;Revise Using Feedback&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;font color=&quot;#ffffff&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 8px;&quot;&gt;User Query: {user_question}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 8px;&quot;&gt;Draft: {draft}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 8px;&quot;&gt;Feedback on your draft: {evaluator_feedback}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 8px;&quot;&gt;Reminding again about the user query: {user_query}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 8px;&quot;&gt;Respond to the user directly. Don&#39;t mention that I provided additional information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-size: 8px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#1ba1e2;strokeColor=#006EAF;fontColor=#ffffff;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1490" y="187.5" width="310" height="100" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="_59ODELO9mQjOOhbrNxv-31" value="&lt;b&gt;FInal Output Answer:&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#ffffff&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;To create a function that multiplies two lists using `map` and `lambda`, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Define a function `mul_list` that takes two lists as arguments.\n2. Use the `map` function to apply a lambda function to the two lists. The lambda function should multiply corresponding elements from the two lists.\n3. Convert the map object to a list to get the final result.\n\nHere is the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef mul_list(list1, list2):\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; \&quot;\&quot;\&quot;\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; This function multiplies two lists using map and lambda function.\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; \n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; Args:\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; list1 (list): The first list.\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; list2 (list): The second list.\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; \n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; Returns:\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; list: A new list with the multiplied elements.\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; \&quot;\&quot;\&quot;\n&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; return list(map(lambda x, y: x * y, list1, list2))\n\n# Test the function\nprint(mul_list([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]))&amp;nbsp; # Output: [4, 10, 18]\n```\n\nIn this code:\n\n- The `mul_list` function takes two lists `list1` and `list2` as arguments.\n- The `map` function applies a lambda function to the two lists. The lambda function takes two arguments `x` and `y` and returns their product `x * y`.\n- The `list` function is used to convert the map object to a list.\n- The function returns the new list with the multiplied elements.\n\nWhen you run this code with the test case `mul_list([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])`, it will output `[4, 10, 18]`, which are the products of the corresponding elements from the two input lists.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#0050ef;fontColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#001DBC;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1328.75" y="327.5" width="632.5" height="235" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
